@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.800 = private unnamed_addr constant [677 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `list()` calls that take unnecessary list or tuple literals as\0Aarguments.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt's unnecessary to use a list or tuple literal within a `list()` call,\0Asince there is a literal syntax for these types.\0A\0AIf a list literal is passed in, then the outer call to `list()` should be\0Aremoved. Otherwise, if a tuple literal is passed in, then it should be\0Arewritten as a list literal.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Alist([1, 2])\0Alist((1, 2))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A[1, 2]\0A[1, 2]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may occasionally drop comments\0Awhen rewriting the call. In most cases, though, comments will be preserved.\0A", align 1
