@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.991 = private unnamed_addr constant [1538 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for assignments that can be replaced with augmented assignment\0Astatements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the right-hand side of an assignment statement consists of a binary\0Aoperation in which one operand is the same as the assignment target,\0Ait can be rewritten as an augmented assignment. For example, `x = x + 1`\0Acan be rewritten as `x += 1`.\0A\0AWhen performing such an operation, an augmented assignment is more concise\0Aand idiomatic.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AIn some cases, this rule will not detect assignments in which the target\0Ais on the right-hand side of a binary operation (e.g., `x = y + x`, as\0Aopposed to `x = x + y`), as such operations are not commutative for\0Acertain data types, like strings.\0A\0AFor example, `x = \22prefix-\22 + x` is not equivalent to `x += \22prefix-\22`,\0Awhile `x = 1 + x` is equivalent to `x += 1`.\0A\0AIf the type of the left-hand side cannot be trivially inferred, the rule\0Awill ignore the assignment.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ax = x + 1\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ax += 1\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as augmented assignments have\0Adifferent semantics when the target is a mutable data type, like a list or\0Adictionary.\0A\0AFor example, consider the following:\0A\0A```python\0Afoo = [1]\0Abar = foo\0Afoo = foo + [2]\0Aassert (foo, bar) == ([1, 2], [1])\0A```\0A\0AIf the assignment is replaced with an augmented assignment, the update\0Aoperation will apply to both `foo` and `bar`, as they refer to the same\0Aobject:\0A\0A```python\0Afoo = [1]\0Abar = foo\0Afoo += [2]\0Aassert (foo, bar) == ([1, 2], [1, 2])\0A```\0A", align 1
