@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1063 = private unnamed_addr constant [2187 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__slots__` definitions that are not ordered according to a\0A[natural sort](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AConsistency is good. Use a common convention for this special variable\0Ato make your code more readable and idiomatic.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Dog:\0A    __slots__ = \22name\22, \22breed\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Dog:\0A    __slots__ = \22breed\22, \22name\22\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe in three situations.\0A\0AFirstly, the fix is unsafe if there are any comments that take up\0Aa whole line by themselves inside the `__slots__` definition, for example:\0A```py\0Aclass Foo:\0A    __slots__ = [\0A        # eggy things\0A        \22duck_eggs\22,\0A        \22chicken_eggs\22,\0A        # hammy things\0A        \22country_ham\22,\0A        \22parma_ham\22,\0A    ]\0A```\0A\0AThis is a common pattern used to delimit categories within a class's slots,\0Abut it would be out of the scope of this rule to attempt to maintain these\0Acategories when applying a natural sort to the items of `__slots__`.\0A\0ASecondly, the fix is also marked as unsafe if there are more than two\0A`__slots__` items on a single line and that line also has a trailing\0Acomment, since here it is impossible to accurately gauge which item the\0Acomment should be moved with when sorting `__slots__`:\0A```py\0Aclass Bar:\0A    __slots__ = [\0A        \22a\22, \22c\22, \22e\22,  # a comment\0A        \22b\22, \22d\22, \22f\22,  # a second  comment\0A    ]\0A```\0A\0ALastly, this rule's fix is marked as unsafe whenever Ruff can detect that\0Acode elsewhere in the same file reads the `__slots__` variable in some way\0Aand the `__slots__` variable is not assigned to a set. This is because the\0Aorder of the items in `__slots__` may have semantic significance if the\0A`__slots__` of a class is being iterated over, or being assigned to another\0Avalue.\0A\0AIn the vast majority of other cases, this rule's fix is unlikely to\0Acause breakage; as such, Ruff will otherwise mark this rule's fix as\0Asafe. However, note that (although it's rare) the value of `__slots__`\0Acould still be read by code outside of the module in which the\0A`__slots__` definition occurs, in which case this rule's fix could\0Atheoretically cause breakage.\0A", align 1
